    {
      "keystrokes": "head -c 100 image.ppm | xxd | head -20\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "There's image.ppm (1.44MB), a high_res.ppm (648MB - huge), an `orig` binary (likely the original renderer), main.py, install.sh, and a .venv. The `orig` binary is likely the program that generated the image, which would tell me exactly what algorithm to replicate. No xxd or file command. Let me check main.py, install.sh, and the binary.",
  "plan": "Read main.py, install.sh, pyproject.toml to understand intent. Then examine the orig binary (it's an ELF, probably the renderer). The PPM header can be read with od. Let me gather info. The high_res.ppm likely is a higher resolution version of the same image, also a clue. Let me first read the text files and check the PPM header with od.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "cat main.py\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "cat install.sh\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "cat pyproject.toml\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "head -c 50 image.ppm | od -c | head\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "image.ppm is P6 (binary) 800x600, max 255, RGB. File size 1440015 = 15-byte header + 800*600*3, matching exactly. The first pixel is (159, 197, 255) - bluish, with many repeated values in early rows. There's an `orig` binary (likely the original renderer) and a huge high_res.ppm (648MB) which is probably a higher-resolution render of the SAME image. I need to figure out the generation algorithm. Let me inspect the `orig` binary's strings and high_res.ppm's dimensions, and use Python to analyze pixel structure.",
  "plan": "1. Get high_res.ppm header dimensions. 2. Run strings on `orig` to understand the algorithm. 3. Confirm Python availability. Then analyze image.ppm pixel data to reverse-engineer the function.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "head -c 40 high_res.ppm | od -c | head\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "strings orig | head -60\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "ls -la .venv/bin/ | head; which python3\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "Key finding: the `orig` binary renders a HIGH-resolution image (8800x6600, P3 ASCII) then downsamples with ffmpeg `scale=800:600` to produce reconstructed.ppm. So image.ppm is the result of: algorithm(x,y) -> high-res pixels -> ffmpeg bicubic downsample to 800x600. The math functions used are floor, sqrt, fmax, fmin. The binary is NOT stripped (shows orig.c, function names) so I can disassemble to extract the exact formula. image.ppm is P6 800x600 RGB. My C program must reproduce this without reading image.ppm, output to reconstructed.ppm. I need to (1) extract the exact algorithm from the binary, and (2) decide whether to render hi-res + downsample or just compute at 800x600. Since 0.8 L2 similarity is the threshold, I can render full-res and box-downsample, or even compute the per-pixel formula and approximate the downsample. Let me disassemble the binary.",
  "plan": "Check available tools (objdump, gdb, python3 with numpy). Then disassemble `orig` to find the render formula and the downsampling relationship. Also examine more strings for constant values. I'll start by checking tools and dumping symbols.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "which objdump gdb nm readelf python3\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "strings orig | grep -iE '[0-9]\\.[0-9]|PI|M_PI|sin|cos|sqrt|noise|fract|fbm' | head -40\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "nm orig 2>/dev/null | head -40\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "Critical insight: `orig` renders a high-res image (8800x6600 P3) then uses ffmpeg `scale=800:600` to produce the 800x600 output. The binary is not stripped - symbols visible: allocate_image, free_image, is_in_shadow, main. I need to extract the exact per-pixel formula from the disassembly. Functions include is_in_shadow and main presumably does the rendering. Let me get the full symbol list and disassemble the rendering logic.",
  "plan": "1. Get full symbol list. 2. Disassemble `main`, `is_in_shadow`, and any render function to a file. 3. Look for the pixel computation and constants. I'll write the disassembly to a file and inspect it.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "nm orig | grep -iE ' T | t ' \n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "objdump -d -M intel orig > /tmp/disasm.txt 2>&1; wc -l /tmp/disasm.txt\n",
      "duration": 0.5
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "grep -nE 'is_in_shadow|render|pixel|compute|color|gradient|noise|<.*>:' /tmp/disasm.txt | head -40\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "This is a ray tracer! Functions: vector_* (math), ray_create/ray_at, sphere_intersect, plane_intersect, sky_color, is_in_shadow, trace, write_image, allocate_image, main. The pipeline: render a high-res 8800x6600 image then ffmpeg downsamples to 800x600. To match, I need to extract the exact algorithm (camera, geometry, lights, sky, shadows) from disassembly and replicate it. For fidelity, I'll supersample (e.g., 16x16 per pixel) in my C program which approximates the high-res downsample. First I need the disassembly of the key functions. Let me extract them.",
  "plan": "Find line numbers of each function in /tmp/disasm.txt, then print sky_color, is_in_shadow, sphere_intersect, plane_intersect, trace, and main to understand the exact math and constants.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "grep -n '<.*>:$' /tmp/disasm.txt\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    }
  ]
}

